{"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-78","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"78","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the United States Fish and Wildlife Service relating to \"Endangered and Threatened Wildlife and Plants; Endangered Species Status for the San Francisco Bay-Delta Distinct Population Segment of the Longfin Smelt\".","introducedDate":"2025-03-21","latestActionDate":"2025-05-05","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate.","policyArea":"Animals","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","California","Congressional oversight","Department of the Interior","Endangered and threatened species","Fishes"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000578","cosponsorBioguideIds":["C000059","F000480","I000056","K000397","M001177","V000129","K000401","O000019"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution nullifies the rule issued by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service titled Endangered and Threatened Wildlife and Plants; Endangered Species Status for the San Francisco Bay-Delta Distinct Population Segment of the Longfin Smelt and published on July 30, 2024. The rule lists the San Francisco Bay-Delta distinct population segment of longfin smelt, a fish species of the Pacific Coast, as an endangered species. Thus, the joint resolution removes protection for the species under the Endangered Species Act of 1973.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/78","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hjres/BILLSTATUS-119hjres78.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:17.096Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hjres-78","plainSummary":"This resolution would overturn a federal rule that listed a specific population of longfin smelt fish in the San Francisco Bay-Delta region as endangered. If passed, the fish would lose protections under the Endangered Species Act that were granted in July 2024. The resolution uses a congressional review process that allows lawmakers to nullify recently issued federal regulations.","industries":[{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Endangered species protections in the Bay-Delta region directly affect water allocation for agricultural irrigation in California's Central Valley."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Water management and hydroelectric operations in the Bay-Delta system face regulatory constraints when species are listed as endangered."},{"industry":"waste-water","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Water utilities and water districts must comply with endangered species protections that affect pumping, diversion, and discharge operations in the Bay-Delta."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Development projects near Bay-Delta waterways face additional permitting requirements and habitat mitigation when endangered species are present."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"29027ec95e2e3c8696233d78","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:47:41.737Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would cancel the rule issued by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service titled Endangered and Threatened Wildlife and Plants; Endangered Species Status for the San Francisco Bay-Delta Distinct Population Segment of the Longfin Smelt and published on July 30, 2024.","whoItTouches":"People who keep animals, and people who work with them.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in how animals must be treated. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hjres-78","bioguideId":"F000480","cycle":2026,"score":0.02993303042113592,"matches":[{"industry":"agr